the united states and germany has struck  a deal to resolve their dispute over the  nordstream 2  project washington has long been opposed  to the gas pipeline connecting russia to  germany  the biden administration has now agreed  to allow its completion without imposing  further sanctions though it remains  opposed to the project  it hasn't even been completed yet the  north stream  ii pipeline has been a major thorn in  the side of u.s  german relations the project will  provide a second pipeline under the  baltic sea  doubling the amount of gas russia can  supply directly to germany  the u.s fears that it'll give russia too  much power over european gas supplies  it also sees the new pipeline as a  security threat  as russia could shut down another  pipeline supplying ukraine and other  nations  we continue to oppose nordstrom 2  we continue to view it as a kremlin  geopolitical project whose  goal is to expand russia's influence  over europe's energy resources we  continue to believe it's a bad deal  for germany it's a bad deal for ukraine  the german chancellor has always framed  the project as a commercial venture  and has tried to deflect opposition from  allies  but she was also determined to resolve  the issue before she leaves office this  autumn  it was one of the topics she discussed  with u.s president joe biden last week  my view on north stream 2 has been  known for some time good friends can  disagree  and but by the time i became president  it was 90 completed and imposing  sanctions did not seem to make any sense  so now a compromise has been reached in  a joint statement washington agreed to  suspend all sanctions and tolerate the  new pipeline  germany in turn is committed to impose  sanctions on russia  if moscow uses the pipeline as a  political weapon  it also agreed to use its leverage to  help extend a gas deal  between russia and ukraine  but critics say the agreement doesn't go  far enough  and ukraine has already voiced its  discontent  the completion of nordstream 2 however  is now all but a  certainty with 98 of the pipeline built  it is set to go into operation later  this year  well for more let's bring in dws chief  political correspondent melinda crane  melinda is this deal a foreign policy  victory for chancellor mackel at the end  of her time in office  absolutely it is she has remained firmly  committed to this project  in the face of opposition not only from  several u.s presidents but also from  the u.s congress and even from some  members of her own  party she admits or she admitted  fairly recently that this project is  not only economic or energy related but  also  geopolitical but she actually sees  geopolitical  benefits in nordstream too she believes  that by keeping these  economic and commercial trade links  open germany and russia foster a degree  of interdependence  that ultimately keeps lines of  communication and diplomatic relations  open so the chancellor essentially has  gotten what she wants the project will  be completed there will not be any more  u.s  sanctions against participants in the  project and germany has made  moderate concessions at best  it will spend money but not a whole lot  of money  trying to help ukraine transform its  energy sector to reduce its  dependence on gas and fossil fuels and  it will  also agree to get tough on russia if  russia uses this new leverage coercively  against uh against ukraine but these are  pretty broad declarations of intention  and otherwise germany hasn't given up  all that much i think what we're seeing  is how very much the u.s wants germany  on its side  as it looks to get harder and tougher  with china  now germany has pledged to impose  sanctions on russia  if moscow uses the pipeline as leverage  against countries like  ukraine how realistic is that  that germany would impose those  sanctions perhaps even unilaterally  i think it's realistic enough germany  has imposed plenty of sanctions  against russia and and various actors in  russia over the past few years naturally  after russia annexed  crimea but also after the poisoning of  sergey skripal  and also in regard uh to the treatment  of alexey navalny the opposition leader  in russia  but the real question is how realistic  is it to think that any of that  will change russia's behavior certainly  we haven't seen much evidence  that russia responds to sanctions now  germany  will have a new chancellor after  september's elections melinda  chancellor mackel is no longer standing  how likely is it that the next  government will  change his position on nordstrom 2.  that depends on how strong the role of  the green party is  in the next government the green party's  chancellor candidate annalina baerbach  has made it very  clear that she would take a harder  stance  vis-a-vis both russia and china and the  greens are no fans of this pipeline  anyway because of course it's  transporting a fossil fuel and they want  to wean germany off  of fossil fuels should the greens be the  party that provides the next deputy  chancellor perhaps even foreign minister  possibly even chancellor look for some  changes on  nordstream 2. however it's worth noting  that in a recent survey  the majority of germans said that  they're for this project  melinda thank you so much good to see  you our chief political correspondent  melinda crane  you
 

